            </tr>
            <tr style="height:176.7000pt;">
                <td width=777 valign=center colspan=14 style="width:466.7000pt;padding:0.0000pt 5.4000pt 0.0000pt 5.4000pt ;border-left:1.0000pt solid windowtext;
mso-border-left-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;border-right:1.0000pt solid windowtext;mso-border-right-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;
border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;border-bottom:1.0000pt solid windowtext;
mso-border-bottom-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;">
                    <p class=MsoNormal><span style="font-family:仿宋;mso-bidi-font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:12.0000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><font face="仿宋"><p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><strong>Course Requirements:</strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">Finish the theoretical lectures and practical.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">Finish the presentation homework.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">Attendance requirements: Unexcused absences for more than 1/3 of the class hours are not allowed to participate in the course examination.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><strong>Disorders of the Kidney and Urinary Tract (Week 1-4)</strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><strong>Adaptation to Renal Injury</strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To master</u> the kidney adaption to nephron loss.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><strong>Glomerulonephropathies</strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To master</u></span></p>
<ol>
<li><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">Approach to patient with suspected glomerular diseases.</span></li>
<li><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">Clinical and pathological clssification of primary glomerular diseases.</span></li>
<li><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">Pathogenesis, pathological and clinical presentation, diagnosis and treatment of acute poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis, IgA nephropathy and diabetic nephropathy.</span></li>
</ol>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To be familiar with</u></span></p>
<ol>
<li><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">Common secondary glomerular diseases: diabetic nephropathy, small vessel vasculitis, lupus nephritis</span></li>
<li><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">Renal pathology</span></li>
</ol>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><strong>Urinary Tract Infections</strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To master</u> the typical clinical manifestations, diagnosis, differential diagnosis and therapeutic principles of acute and chronic pyelonephritis.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">To grasp the etiology, contagious ways and infection-relative factors of pyelonephritis.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">To grasp the main laboratory diagnostic methods of pyelonephritis.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><strong>Acute kidney injury </strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To master</u> the RIFLE and KDIGO criteria of AKI classification and AKI staging.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To be familiar with</u> 1) common causes of AKI; 2) clinical approach to the diagnosis of AKI; 3) Clinical indications for emergent dialysis.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To know</u> the basic concepts and indications of continuous renal replacement therapy.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><strong>Chronic kidney disease</strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To master</u> the definition and stage of CKD, clinical manifestations and treatment of CKD.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To be familiar with</u> epidemiology of CKD, causes and pathophysiology of CKD.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To be familiar with</u> CKD assessment and management（KDIGO guideline）</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><strong>Fluid and Electrolyte Disturbances</strong> & <strong>Acidosis and Alkalosis</strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To master</u> the main etiology, pathogenesis, clinical manifestations, diagnosis and principle of therapy of these clinical cases: inadequate volume, hypernatremia, hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, hypokalemia, metabolic acidosis and metabolic alkalosis.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To master </u>the methods to calculate the dosage of intravenous pharmaceutical applied in correcting the disorder of water and electrolytes.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">To grasp the main cause, pathogenesis, clinical manifestations, diagnostic and therapeutic principles of these clinical cases: water excess, water intoxication, respiratory acidosis and respiratory alkalosis. To understand the pathogenesis and diagnosis of mixed acid-base disorder.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><strong>Disorders of hemotologic System (Week 4-7)</strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><strong>Anemias</strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To master</u> basic information of anemia, including iron deficiency anemia, aplastic anemia, and hemolytic anemia.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To be familiar with</u> etiology, clinical features, laboratory findings, diagnosis and treatment of every kind of anemia described above.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><strong>Acute and Chronic Myeloid Leukemia</strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To master </u>the clinical presentation, the diagnosis and the general rules of treatment of leukemia</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To be familiar with</u> the classification of leukemia and chemotherapy</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">To understand the etiology of leukemia</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><strong>Malignancies of Lymphoid Cells</strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To master</u> the definition, diagnosis and Ann Arbor Staging System of lymphoma.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To be familiar with</u> International Prognostic Index and treatment strategy for lymphoma.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><strong>Multiple Myeloma</strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To master</u> the clinical manifestations, diagnosis of multiple myeloma.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To be familiar with</u> the classification, staging and treatment principles of multiple myeloma.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">To understand the etiology, pathogenesis and prognosis of multiple myeloma.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><strong>Disorders of Coagulation and Thrombosis</strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To master: </u></span></p>
<ol>
<li><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">The coagulation cascade has two pathways which lead to fibrin formation;</span></li>
<li><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">Factors VIII and IX deficiency;</span></li>
<li><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC);</span></li>
<li><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">Platelet disorders ,Immune Thrombocytopenic (ITP);</span></li>
<li><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">Clinical Tests of coagulation and thrombosis.</span></li>
</ol>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To be familiar with: </u></span></p>
<ol>
<li><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">Venous and arterial thrombosis and embolism;</span></li>
<li><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">Thrombotic thrombocytopenia purpura (TTP);</span></li>
<li><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">Procoagulants and Anticoagulants.</span></li>
</ol>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><strong>Endocrinology and Metabolism (Week 7-11)</strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><strong>Principles of Endocrinology</strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To master</u> the definition and classification of hormone; the principle of hypothalamus - pituitary - hormone axis; the principle of the diagnosis of endocrine diseases.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To be familiar with </u>the mechanisms of hormone action; the classification of endocrine diseases; the principle of the treatment of endocrine diseases.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><strong>Diseases of Thyroid Gland </strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To master</u> the etiology classification, clinical manifestation, laboratory evaluation, diagnosis and differential diagnosis and treatment of hyperthyroidism and hypothyroidism</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To be familiar with</u></span></p>
<ol>
<li><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">Basic knowledge about the thyroid gland</span></li>
<li><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">The overview of the thyroid disorders</span></li>
<li><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">Other thyroid disease such as subacute thyroiditis, thyroid nodular</span></li>
</ol>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><strong>Disorders of the Adrenal Cortex </strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To master </u>the etiological classification and clinical manifestation of Cushing’s syndrome and primary aldosteronism, and the main functional and localization diagnostic tests of both diseases.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To be familiar with</u> the differential diagnosis and treatment principles of Cushing’s syndrome and primary aldosteronism.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><strong>Diabetes Mellitus </strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To master</u> the diagnosis of diabetes; the different types of diabetes and the treatment of diabetes.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To be familiar with</u> the diagnosis and treatment of DKA and the pathophysiology and mechanism of diabetes and related complications.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><strong>Disorders of the Immune System, Connective Tissue, and Joints (Week 12-14)</strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><strong>Principles of Rheumatology</strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To master</u> the definition and core contents of rheumatic diseases.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To be familiar with</u> common symptoms and signs of rheumatic diseases; classification and clinical significance of autoantibodies related to rheumatic diseases; commonly used imaging modalities (including X-ray, CT, MRI, ultrasound) and their application value in the diagnosis of rheumatic diseases; main treatment alternatives (pharmacological therapy, physical therapy, surgical intervention, etc.) and their clinical indications.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><strong>Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE)</strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To master:</u></span></p>
<ol>
<li><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">Systemic clinical manifestations (involving skin and mucous membranes, joints, kidneys, cardiovascular system, respiratory system, nervous system, hematological system, etc.).</span></li>
<li><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">Key laboratory examinations, with special emphasis on immune disorders (autoantibodies such as ANA, anti-dsDNA, anti-Sm, anti-SSA/Ro, anti-SSB/La; complement C3, C4 levels; immune complex detection, etc.).</span></li>
<li><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">International diagnostic criteria (e.g., ACR/EULAR classification criteria) and clinical diagnostic principles.</span></li>
<li><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">Disease activity indices (e.g., SLEDAI-2K) and evaluation methods.</span></li>
<li><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">Indices suggesting chronic organ damage (e.g., renal scarring, pulmonary fibrosis, cardiovascular complications, etc.).</span></li>
<li><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">Overall therapeutic principles (including patient education, lifestyle management, treatment goals) and individualized pharmaceutical therapy (glucocorticoids, antimalarials, conventional synthetic DMARDs, biologic DMARDs, targeted synthetic DMARDs, etc.), as well as dosage adjustment, adverse reaction monitoring, and long-term management.</span></li>
</ol>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To understand: </u>Etiological factors (genetic factors, environmental triggers such as infections, ultraviolet radiation, drugs; hormonal factors, etc.) and pathological mechanisms (immune complex deposition, abnormal activation of B cells and T cells, defective immune regulation, etc.)</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><strong>Rheumatoid Arthritis</strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">To master:</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">1.Typical clinical features: Symmetrical polyarthritis (involving small joints such as hands, wrists, feet; joint swelling, pain, tenderness, stiffness, functional impairment); extra-articular manifestations (rheumatoid nodules, interstitial lung disease, cardiovascular involvement, hematological abnormalities, etc.).</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">2.Pathologic characteristics: Synovitis (synovial hyperplasia, inflammatory cell infiltration), pannus formation, cartilage and bone destruction, joint deformity.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">3.Key autoantibodies: Rheumatoid factor (RF), anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide (anti-CCP) antibodies, and their clinical significance (diagnostic value, prognostic implication).</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">To be familiar with:</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">1.Updated international diagnosis criteria (e.g., 2010 ACR/EULAR classification criteria) and differential diagnosis (distinguishing from osteoarthritis, psoriatic arthritis, gout, SLE, etc.).</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">2.Disease activity evaluation tools (e.g., DAS28, CDAI, SDAI) and assessment of functional status (e.g., HAQ-DI).</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">3.Comprehensive treatment alternatives: Non-pharmacological therapy (patient education, physical therapy, occupational therapy, exercise training); pharmacological therapy (non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, glucocorticoids, conventional synthetic DMARDs, biologic DMARDs, targeted synthetic DMARDs); surgical treatment (joint replacement, synovectomy, etc.) for advanced cases</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><strong>Spondyloarthritides</strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">To master:</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">1.Classification of seronegative spondyloarthropathies: Including ankylosing spondylitis (AS), psoriatic arthritis (PsA), reactive arthritis (ReA), enteropathic arthritis (EA), undifferentiated spondyloarthropathy, etc.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">2.Common characteristics of the group: Seronegativity for RF and anti-CCP antibodies; association with HLA-B27; predilection for axial skeleton (sacroiliac joints, spine) and peripheral joints; extra-articular manifestations (uveitis, psoriasis, inflammatory bowel disease, enthesitis, etc.).</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">3.Comparison of clinical characteristics among different subtypes: Differences in age of onset, joint involvement patterns (axial vs. peripheral), extra-articular manifestations, and prognosis.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">For Ankylosing Spondylitis (AS) specifically:</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">To master:</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">1.Clinical manifestations: Inflammatory low back pain (insidious onset, morning stiffness &gt;1 hour, improvement with activity), sacroiliac joint pain, spinal stiffness and fusion, peripheral arthritis, enthesitis (e.g., Achilles tendinitis, plantar fasciitis), uveitis, etc.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">2.Laboratory examinations: HLA-B27 typing, inflammatory markers (ESR, CRP), and exclusion of other diseases.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">3.Pathological features: Sacroiliitis (synovitis, cartilage destruction, bone erosion, bony fusion), spondylitis, enthesitis.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">4.Therapeutic strategies: Non-pharmacological therapy (patient education, regular exercise, posture management); pharmacological therapy (non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs as first-line, DMARDs for peripheral arthritis, biologic DMARDs targeting TNF, IL-17, etc.); surgical intervention for severe spinal deformity or joint damage.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><strong>Hyperuricemia and Gout</strong><strong><br></strong>To master:</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">1.Definition: Hyperuricemia (normal uric acid levels, classification of hyperuricemia: primary vs. secondary); gout (definition as a disease caused by monosodium urate crystal deposition, classification: primary vs. secondary).</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">2.Clinical manifestations of gout: Acute gouty arthritis; intercritical gou; chronic tophaceous gout; gouty nephropathy.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">To be familiar with:</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">1.Imaging modalities: X-ray findings; ultrasound (double contour sign, tophi, synovitis, tenosynovitis); dual-energy CT (detection of urate crystals) and their diagnostic value.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">2.Diagnosis criteria and key points of differential diagnosis.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">3.Treatment principles and methods:</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">Management of hyperuricemia: Lifestyle modification (dietary control, limiting alcohol and high-fructose beverages, weight loss, adequate hydration); urate-lowering therapy (xanthine oxidase inhibitors, uricosuric agents, uricase inhibitors) and target uric acid levels.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">Treatment of acute gouty arthritis: Anti-inflammatory drugs (non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, colchicine, glucocorticoids) and their usage, dosage, and contraindications.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">Management of chronic gout: Tophus treatment, prevention of acute attacks, protection of renal function.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><strong>Poisoning and Diseases of Critical Care Medicine (Week 14-16)</strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><strong>Poisoning And Drug Overdosage</strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">To master the pathogenesis of common poisons, including the molecular mechanisms of toxic effects on target organs, metabolic disorders induced by poisoning, and pathological changes.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">To master the clinical manifestations and diagnostic principles of acute and chronic poisoning: recognize typical symptoms and signs of different poison exposures, master the steps of clinical diagnosis (history collection, physical examination, laboratory tests), and differentiate poisoning from other similar diseases.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">To master the treatment principles of poisoning: proficiently apply general treatment measures (decontamination, toxin elimination, supportive care) and specific antidote therapies and formulate individualized treatment plans for common poisoning cases.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">To understand the specific properties, exposure situations, absorption, and metabolism of common poisons: including chemical and physical properties of poisons (e.g., volatility, solubility), common exposure routes (oral, inhalation, dermal, injection), processes of absorption, distribution, metabolism, and excretion in the human body, and factors affecting toxic effects.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><strong>Principles of Critical Care Medicine</strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To master</u> the major tasks of critical care medicine</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To be familiar with</u> the difference between ICU and other wards</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><strong>Shock</strong></span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To master</u> the definitions and categorization of shock.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To be familiar with </u>the pathogenesis and pathophysiology of shock.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"><u>To be familiar with </u>the initial support in shock.</span></p></font></span><b style="mso-bidi-font-weight:normal"><span style="font-family:'Times New Roman';mso-fareast-font-family:仿宋;mso-ansi-font-weight:bold;
font-size:10.5000pt;mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><o:p></o:p></span></b></p>
                </td>
            </tr>
